# Operational iOS SSL Pinning Bypass — Working Playbook

## Context

You're testing iOS apps in authorized scope (bug bounty / pentest / CTF / research on
apps you control). SSL pinning blocks your intercept proxy (Burp / mitmproxy / Caido)
from seeing TLS traffic, even after installing your CA. This playbook is the
operational sequence I run: cheapest tooling first, surgical Frida hooks next,
binary patching as the last resort. It is built around real-world failure modes
(NSURLSession + custom `URLSessionDelegate`, AFNetworking/Alamofire pin sets,
TrustKit, custom C/Swift pinning, Flutter/Xamarin/RN stacks, CFNetwork).

Assume jailbroken device for production runs; non-jailbroken path is documented for
when only an IPA is available.

---

## Phase 0 — Prerequisites

### Device

- **Preferred:** iPhone with `palera1n` (A11 and below, iOS 15–17) or `Dopamine`
  (A12+, iOS 15–16.6.1), or `checkra1n` for legacy hardware.
- SSH over USB via `iproxy 2222 22` (libimobiledevice). Default creds: `root` / `alpine`
  (rotate immediately after first boot).
- Cydia / Sileo / Zebra installed.

### On-device packages (via Sileo/Zebra/apt)

- `frida-server` (from build.frida.re repo) — match version to your host Frida.
- `Filza` or `NewTerm 3` — file browsing + on-device shell.
- `SSL Kill Switch 3` (Julio Verne / evilpenguin) — drop-in for iOS 14–17. (SSL
  Kill Switch 2 is dead past iOS 13.)
- `Liberty Lite (Beta)` or `Choicy` — to prevent jailbreak detection from killing
  the app before pinning even matters.
- `appinst` / `TrollStore` for sideloading patched IPAs without re-signing.

### Host (Windows in your case, but most tooling is cross-platform)

- Python 3.11+ venv.
- `pip install frida-tools objection`. Match `frida` and `frida-server` versions
  exactly — version mismatch silently breaks `Interceptor`.
- `Burp Suite` or `mitmproxy` (`pip install mitmproxy`). Configure proxy listener
  on `0.0.0.0:8080`.
- Install Burp/mitmproxy CA on device: Settings → General → VPN & Device
  Management → trust profile → Settings → General → About → Certificate Trust
  Settings → enable for the CA. Without the second step root CA is installed but
  *not trusted for TLS* — common silent failure.
- `class-dump`, `otool`, `lldb`, `Hopper`/`IDA`/`Ghidra` for static work.
- `frida-ios-dump` (AloneMonkey) to pull decrypted `.ipa` from a jailbroken device.

### Connectivity sanity check

Before touching pinning, prove the proxy + CA chain works on a *non-pinned* app
(Safari hitting https://example.com should show no warning, request should appear
in proxy). If Safari fails, your CA install is wrong — fix that before blaming
pinning.

