## Phase 2 — Bypass methods, in escalation order

### Method A — SSL Kill Switch 3 (try this first, 30 seconds)

For ~70% of apps using stock AFNetworking, ATS `NSPinnedDomains`, or no pinning
at all (in case ATS is just blocking your CA):

1. Install via Sileo: `https://repo.thecaptain989.com/` or `evilpenguinblog`'s
   repo.
2. Settings → SSL Kill Switch 3 → enable for the target bundle ID.
3. Force-close and relaunch the app.
4. Replay the flow with proxy attached.

If traffic appears: done. If not, go to Method B. SSL Kill Switch 3 does not
defeat: TrustKit with custom callback, Flutter, Cronet, manually-implemented
`SecTrustEvaluate` checks. That's what Frida is for.

### Method B — Objection (Frida wrapper, 2 minutes)

```
# on host, with frida-server running on device
objection -g <bundle.id> explore
ios sslpinning disable
```

`ios sslpinning disable` patches: `SecTrustEvaluate`, `SecTrustEvaluateWithError`,
`NSURLSession` delegate `didReceiveChallenge`, AFNetworking's
`setSSLPinningMode:`, TrustKit's `TSKPinningValidator.evaluateTrust:forHostname:`.
It's a single command; try it before writing custom Frida.

Verify in proxy. If still pinning (Alamofire's `PublicKeysTrustEvaluator`, Flutter,
custom BoringSSL), drop to Method C.

### Method C — Targeted Frida hooks

Run from host:
```
frida -U -f <bundle.id> -l bypass.js --no-pause
```

Use the `bypass.js` patterns below. Combine — they don't conflict.

#### C.1 — Generic Apple ObjC + Sec* trust APIs

```javascript
// bypass.js — generic iOS trust bypass
if (ObjC.available) {
  try {
    // NSURLSession delegate path used by most modern Swift/ObjC code
    var resolver = new ApiResolver('objc');
    resolver.enumerateMatches(
      '-[* URLSession:didReceiveChallenge:completionHandler:]',
      {
        onMatch: function (m) {
          Interceptor.attach(m.address, {
            onEnter: function (args) {
              // self=args[0], _cmd=args[1], session=args[2], challenge=args[3], handler=args[4]
              this.challenge = new ObjC.Object(args[3]);
              this.handler   = new ObjC.Block(args[4]);
            },
            onLeave: function () {
              try {
                var protSpace = this.challenge.protectionSpace();
                var trust = protSpace.serverTrust();
                if (trust && !trust.isNull()) {
                  var cred = ObjC.classes.NSURLCredential
                    .credentialForTrust_(trust);
                  // NSURLSessionAuthChallengeUseCredential = 0
                  this.handler.implementation(0, cred);
                }
              } catch (e) { /* fall through to original */ }
            }
          });
        },
        onComplete: function () {}
      }
    );
  } catch (e) { console.log('[!] NSURLSession hook failed: ' + e); }
}

// Sec* C API path — catches Cronet, custom CFNetwork, some Swift stacks
var SecTrustEvaluate = Module.findExportByName(null, 'SecTrustEvaluate');
if (SecTrustEvaluate) {
  Interceptor.replace(SecTrustEvaluate, new NativeCallback(function (trust, resultPtr) {
    // kSecTrustResultProceed = 1
    Memory.writeU32(resultPtr, 1);
    return 0; // errSecSuccess
  }, 'int', ['pointer', 'pointer']));
}

var SecTrustEvaluateWithError = Module.findExportByName(null, 'SecTrustEvaluateWithError');
if (SecTrustEvaluateWithError) {
  Interceptor.replace(SecTrustEvaluateWithError, new NativeCallback(function (trust, errorPtr) {
    if (!errorPtr.isNull()) Memory.writePointer(errorPtr, NULL);
    return 1; // true => trusted
  }, 'bool', ['pointer', 'pointer']));
}
```

#### C.2 — TrustKit

```javascript
if (ObjC.available && ObjC.classes.TSKPinningValidator) {
  var V = ObjC.classes.TSKPinningValidator;
  V['- evaluateTrust:forHostname:'].implementation = function () {
    // TSKTrustEvaluationResult: TSKTrustEvaluationSuccess = 0
    return 0;
  };
  // newer TrustKit splits into TSKTrustDecision: TSKTrustDecisionShouldAllow = 0
  if (V['- handleChallenge:forHostname:completionHandler:']) {
    V['- handleChallenge:forHostname:completionHandler:'].implementation =
      function (challenge, hostname, completion) {
        var cred = ObjC.classes.NSURLCredential
          .credentialForTrust_(challenge.protectionSpace().serverTrust());
        new ObjC.Block(completion).implementation(0, cred);
        return true;
      };
  }
}
```

#### C.3 — AFNetworking (when SSL Kill Switch isn't installed)

```javascript
if (ObjC.available && ObjC.classes.AFSecurityPolicy) {
  var P = ObjC.classes.AFSecurityPolicy;
  P['- evaluateServerTrust:forDomain:'].implementation = function () { return true; };
  P['- setSSLPinningMode:'].implementation = function () { /* swallow */ };
  P['- setAllowInvalidCertificates:'].implementation = function () { /* swallow */ };
}
```

#### C.4 — Alamofire (Swift name-mangled — bind on demangled selectors)

Alamofire's `PublicKeysTrustEvaluator.evaluate(_:forHost:)` is hard to address by
mangled symbol across versions. Hook the C trust APIs (C.1 already does) *and*
disable Alamofire's `ServerTrustManager` decision:

```javascript
// Catch Swift-bridged calls that end in SecTrust* — covered by C.1.
// For Alamofire's PinnedCertificatesTrustEvaluator specifically:
var alamo = Process.findModuleByName('Alamofire');
if (alamo) {
  alamo.enumerateSymbols().forEach(function (s) {
    if (/TrustEvaluator.*evaluate/.test(s.name)) {
      try {
        Interceptor.replace(s.address, new NativeCallback(function () {
          return 1; // succeed
        }, 'bool', ['pointer','pointer','pointer']));
      } catch (e) {}
    }
  });
}
```

#### C.5 — Flutter (libflutter.dylib / Flutter.framework)

Flutter ships its own BoringSSL and ignores the system trust store entirely. The
target is `ssl_crypto_x509_session_verify_cert_chain`. Offset shifts per Flutter
release — locate by pattern, not by symbol (the symbol is stripped in release).

```javascript
var flutter = Process.findModuleByName('Flutter');
if (flutter) {
  // Pattern for arm64 Flutter 3.x — verify against your build with Ghidra first.
  // Failing that, use Flutter-bypass repo's offset table.
  // Pseudocode: find function, replace with `return 1`.
  // For production, use https://github.com/Tonyzzx/flutter-ssl-bypass-frida
}
```

Realistically: clone `flutter-ssl-bypass-frida` (or `disable-flutter-tls`),
paste in the offset for your Flutter version (`strings Flutter | grep
'Flutter Engine'` to identify), run.

#### C.6 — Cronet / Chromium net stack

Hook `SSL_VERIFY_X509_callback` style entry points or, more practically, the
Cronet-level `URLRequest` delegate. If you see `cronet_*` symbols, this is a
Chromium build — the same `SecTrustEvaluate` hooks don't apply because Cronet
bundles BoringSSL. Use `frida-cronet-bypass` or hook
`Cronet_UrlRequest_*Verify*`.

#### C.7 — Jailbreak detection (preflight)

If the app dies before you can hook, jailbreak detection is the gate. Hook these
first, then load pinning bypass:

```javascript
// File-existence probes
var open = Module.findExportByName(null, 'open');
Interceptor.attach(open, {
  onEnter: function (args) {
    this.path = Memory.readUtf8String(args[0]);
  },
  onLeave: function (retval) {
    if (this.path && /(Cydia|Sileo|MobileSubstrate|frida|cycript|apt|bash|ssh|jailbreak)/i.test(this.path)) {
      retval.replace(-1);
    }
  }
});

// fork() — many JB checks try fork and bail if it works (because sandbox blocks fork)
var fork = Module.findExportByName(null, 'fork');
if (fork) Interceptor.replace(fork, new NativeCallback(function () { return -1; }, 'int', []));

// URL scheme checks (canOpenURL: with cydia://, sileo://)
if (ObjC.available) {
  var UIApp = ObjC.classes.UIApplication;
  UIApp['- canOpenURL:'].implementation = function (url) {
    var s = url.absoluteString().toString();
    if (/(cydia|sileo|undecimus|filza)/i.test(s)) return false;
    return this.canOpenURL_(url);
  };
}
```

### Method D — Non-jailbroken: patch the IPA

When you only have the IPA and no JB device, options in order of effort:

1. **Objection patchipa** — repacks the IPA with `FridaGadget.dylib` injected
   and a load command added. Resign with your dev cert and sideload via Xcode /
   Sideloadly / AltStore. Then run Frida against the gadget.
   ```
   objection patchipa --source Target.ipa --codesign-signature <CERT_HASH>
   ```
2. **Static patch of pinning calls** — open the binary in Hopper/Ghidra, find
   `SecTrustEvaluateWithError`, replace prologue with `MOV X0, #1 ; RET`. For
   AFNetworking, NOP the `evaluateServerTrust:forDomain:` body and force true.
   Resign (`ldid -S` on tweaked binary, then `zip` back into IPA).
3. **`optool`/`insert_dylib`** to add a load command for `libsubstitute.dylib`
   or `FridaGadget.dylib` if patchipa fails.

Resigning is the painful step on Windows — recommend doing it from a macOS VM
(or a Mac in your CI) using `codesign --force --sign <dev-id> --entitlements
Entitlements.plist`. Or use TrollStore on a compatible iOS version (15.0–16.6.1,
17.0) which avoids resigning entirely.

### Method E — Plist / Info.plist tweak for ATS pinning

iOS 14+ `NSPinnedDomains` in `Info.plist`. Patch the IPA:

```xml
<!-- Remove or empty: -->
<key>NSAppTransportSecurity</key>
<dict>
    <key>NSPinnedDomains</key>
    <dict>
        <key>api.target.com</key>
        <dict>
            <key>NSPinnedCAIdentities</key>
            <array>
                <dict><key>SPKI-SHA256-BASE64</key><string>...</string></dict>
            </array>
        </dict>
    </dict>
</dict>
```

Delete the `NSPinnedDomains` subtree, optionally add
`NSAllowsArbitraryLoads=true`, resign, sideload. This only works when the app
relies *solely* on ATS pinning — most production apps stack a code-level check
on top.

